L77: /// Entrypoint used when `#[entrypoint]` is used on a contract struct.
L78: /// Solidity requires specific routing logic for situations in which no function selector
L79: /// matches the input calldata in the form of two different functions named "receive" and
L80: /// "fallback". The purity and type definitions are as follows:
L81: ///
L82: /// - receive takes no input data, returns no data, and is always payable.
L83: /// - fallback offers two possible implementations. It can be either declared without input or
L84: ///   return
L85: //    parameters, or with input bytes calldata and return bytes memory.
L86: //
L87: //  The fallback function MAY be payable. If not payable, then any transactions not matching any
L88: //  other function which send value will revert.
L89: //
L90: //  The order of routing semantics for receive and fallback work as follows:
L91: //
L92: //  - If a receive function exists, it is always called whenever calldata is empty, even if no value
L93: //    is received in the transaction. It is implicitly payable.
L94: //  - Fallback is called when no other function matches a selector. If a receive function is not
L95: //    defined, then calls with no input calldata will be routed to the fallback function.
